{
  "name": "Making Your Home More Usable Across Every Room",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "You want your home to truly work for you, no matter your mobility needs. When you start thinking about whole-home accessibility, a common question is how to make every room usable and which upgrades will give you the most independence. It's easy to get overwhelmed by the scope, but the key is to think about how you move through your entire home. Focus on identifying any barriers to your daily tasks. Are there narrow doorways, high thresholds, or difficult transitions between flooring types? Once you know the problem areas, you can start installing or adjusting features like sturdy handrails on all staircases, grab bars in bathrooms, and raised toilet seats. Better lighting throughout the house can dramatically improve visibility, especially in those tricky spots. Think about reachable storage in the kitchen and other areas, too, so you don't have to strain or use unsafe step stools. The process often happens in phases, starting with simpler, low-cost changes and then moving to more involved projects like converting a tub to a curb-less shower or adding a ramp. Avoiding a room-by-room approach without considering the whole layout is crucial. If your needs go beyond simple safety fixes, especially if multiple rooms or structural changes are involved, a qualified professional can help coordinate everything to ensure a seamless and safe home environment.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    }
  ],
